WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.850
Scale the publishing system by asking which
resource is doing repeated work. If most

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:10.103
requests read public pages, the database may
first benefit from more memory, better queries

00:00:10.103 --> 00:00:15.415
and additional read replicas. A read replica
serves another copy of the records, while edits

00:00:15.415 --> 00:00:17.089
still go through the primary.

00:00:17.089 --> 00:00:22.089
If page construction becomes the bottleneck, add
stateless application instances behind a

00:00:22.089 --> 00:00:27.202
load balancer. Those instances can share the
same authoritative storage because they do not

00:00:27.202 --> 00:00:32.258
require local per-user state to answer the next
request. The progression keeps each change

00:00:32.258 --> 00:00:33.944
connected to a measured limit.

00:00:34.089 --> 00:00:38.480
Adding machines to a tier that is not limiting
throughput will not solve a bottleneck

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:38.996
elsewhere.

00:00:39.089 --> 00:00:44.598
The next step can avoid application work
altogether for eligible requests. Put a response

00:00:44.598 --> 00:00:49.859
cache in front of public HTML pages and keep
media delivery cached as well. Anonymous

00:00:49.859 --> 00:00:55.553
visitors requesting the same page can reuse the
same response, provided the freshness policy

00:00:55.553 --> 00:00:56.234
permits it.

00:00:56.234 --> 00:01:01.672
The architecture now has two filters on origin
demand: media hits bypass object-origin

00:01:01.672 --> 00:01:07.362
retrieval, and HTML hits bypass page
construction and its database queries. Follow a
cache

00:01:07.362 --> 00:01:12.863
hit and a miss as separate paths. A hit is
short; a miss still uses the complete origin

00:01:12.863 --> 00:01:13.180
path.

00:01:13.234 --> 00:01:17.920
The origin must therefore tolerate miss bursts,
cache eviction and recovery instead of

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:21.081
assuming that the average hit rate will always
protect it.

00:01:21.234 --> 00:01:26.534
A large monthly total can sound more demanding
than its rate actually is. The case projects

00:01:26.534 --> 00:01:31.543
roughly four hundred thousand monthly write-
generating events at two hundred times the

00:01:31.543 --> 00:01:36.610
traffic: eighty million events per month.
Dividing by approximately 2.6 million seconds

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:39.638
gives about thirty-one events per second on
average.

00:01:39.734 --> 00:01:44.480
This calculation does not prove that the system
can handle the workload, but it challenges

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:49.067
the assumption that a large audience
automatically requires sharded writes. The
diagram

00:01:49.067 --> 00:01:52.759
should show the conversion from events per month
to events per second.

00:01:52.759 --> 00:01:57.723
Always attach the unit to a number; comparing a
monthly event count directly with a per-

00:01:57.723 --> 00:01:59.866
second device capacity is meaningless.

00:01:59.866 --> 00:02:05.263
Next compare the projection with measurements.
If a current workload uses about ten storage

00:02:05.263 --> 00:02:10.482
operations per second, a simple two-hundred-fold
projection suggests about two thousand,

00:02:10.482 --> 00:02:15.642
subject to the assumption that operations grow
proportionally. CPU observations provide

00:02:15.642 --> 00:02:20.743
another estimate, but multiplying core count by
current spare capacity is only a rough

00:02:20.743 --> 00:02:21.099
bound.

00:02:21.099 --> 00:02:26.547
Lock contention, serial work, memory pressure
and changed cache behavior can break linear

00:02:26.547 --> 00:02:32.119
extrapolation. Likewise, one application event
is not necessarily one database write or one

00:02:32.119 --> 00:02:37.506
storage operation. The purpose of the estimate
is to identify plausible capacity and the

00:02:37.506 --> 00:02:43.139
next experiment, not to replace a load test with
arithmetic that ignores the execution path.
